WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.680
An H100 and an H200 have the same compute.

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.960
The H200 still
generates chat tokens faster.

3
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:08.970
The reason is the bottleneck in decode.

4
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.940
A GPU has two main resources.

5
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.720
Compute is how many
operations it performs per second.

6
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.000
Memory bandwidth is how many bytes per
second it moves from VRAM to its compute units.

7
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.440
Decode produces one
token per forward pass.

8
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.560
Each pass reads every
model weight from VRAM.

9
00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.840
For each sequence, those weights feed
only a small vector-matrix multiplication.

10
00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.760
At low to medium batch sizes, the
compute units finish that math long before the

11
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.140
next weights arrive.

12
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.480
Then they wait.

13
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.220
Arithmetic intensity measures this:
operations performed per byte moved.

14
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.220
An H100 in FP16 can perform
about 295 operations per byte.

15
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:58.320
Decode performs far fewer, so it
sits under the bandwidth ceiling.

16
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.960
That is why the H200 wins.

17
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.170
It moves about 43
percent more bytes per second.

18
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:08.620
Batching helps too, because one read of
the weights then serves many requests.

19
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.700
Pause and predict.

20
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.480
GPU A has twice the compute of GPU B.

21
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.970
GPU B has twice the bandwidth.

22
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.340
You serve chat to a few users at a time.

23
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.920
Which GPU generates
more tokens per second?
